{"qid":"Q1061057","names":["Sultanate of Aceh"],"title":"Sultanate of Aceh","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Sultanate of Aceh was founded around 1496–1516 at the northern tip of Sumatra and rose to become the dominant power of the Strait of Malacca after the Portuguese seizure of Malacca in 1511, positioning itself as the leading Muslim trading state in Southeast Asia. Under Sultan Iskandar Muda (r. 1607–1636) Aceh reached its greatest extent, controlling the pepper-producing ports of Sumatra's west coast and staging attacks on Johor and Portuguese Malacca, while supporting an internationally recognized court of Islamic scholarship. After Iskandar Muda's death Aceh contracted steadily; the Dutch colonial presence eventually circumscribed it, and the Aceh War (1873–1904) ended meaningful Acehnese sovereignty, though a formal sultanate persisted nominally until integration into Indonesia in 1949–1960.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territory on the northern Sumatran coast is well-defined; Aceh's control over subordinate ports along the Sumatran west coast and Malay Peninsula varied greatly by decade and is incompletely mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Aceh Sultanate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aceh_Sultanate"},{"label":"Britannica: Aceh","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Aceh"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Aceh Sultanate","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Ali Mughayat Syah","nativeName":"علي مغايت شاه","roman":"Ali Mughayat Syah","start":1516,"end":1530,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Salahuddin","nativeName":"ص